<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>The Riemann Lebesgue Lemma part 1</title>

 <section xml:id="sec-definingMeasure-s-xref">
  <title>The Riemann Lebesgue lemma</title>
<p> Here are two things you will remember from the unit on the Riemann integral:  
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        Every continuous function is integrable
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        The indicator function for the rationals <m>\chi_\Q</m> is not integrable.  It is also discontinuous at every point in \(\R\).  
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
 </p>

 <p>
  This suggests the following.  Perhaps a function is integrable if and only if it is <i>not too discontinuous</i> whatever that means. </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-">
    <title>The Riemann Lebesgue lemma</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A function <m>f:[a,b]\to \R</m> is integrable over <m>[a,b]</m> if and only if <m>m^*(\discont(f))=0</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  <p workspace="1cm"> Here <m>\discont(f)</m> is the set of all discontinuities of <m>f</m>.  Look back at the definition of the outer measure.  <m>m^*(\discont(f))=0</m> if and only if <m>\dots</m>.
 </p>
 <solution>
  <m>m^*(\discont(f))=0</m> if and only if for every <m>\epsilon\gt 0</m> there is a countable cover <m>\I</m> of <m>\discont(f)</m> by open interval with total length less than <m>\epsilon</m>.
 <solution>

  

</section>


</handout>